    "id": "story",
    "type": "story",
    "provenance": "original",
    "title": {
     "en": "A short story"
    },
    "text": {
     "en": "A bacterium doubles every minute. After 3 minutes there are 2 × 2 × 2 = 8 bacteria. Writing 2 three times is slow, so mathematicians write it as 2 to the power 3."
    }
   },
   {
    "id": "explain",
    "type": "explanation",
    "provenance": "original",
    "keepEnglishTerms": [
     "base",
     "index",
     "product law",
     "negative index"
    ],
    "text": {
     "en": "In a^n, a is the base and n is the index or exponent. It means a multiplied by itself n times. So 2^5 = 2 × 2 × 2 × 2 × 2 = 32 and 10^3 = 1000.\nThe product law says a^m × a^n = a^(m+n) when the base is the same. For example 3^2 × 3^4 = 3^6 = 729. The quotient law says a^m ÷ a^n = a^(m−n), so 5^7 ÷ 5^4 = 5^3 = 125.\nThe power law says (a^m)^n = a^(mn). For example (2^3)^2 = 2^6 = 64. Also (ab)^n = a^n × b^n, so (2 × 5)^2 = 4 × 25 = 100.\nAny non-zero number to the power 0 is 1, so 7^0 = 1. A negative index means the reciprocal, so a^(−n) = 1 ÷ a^n. For example 2^(−3) = 1/8. A fractional index such as a^(1/2) means the square root of a, so 9^(1/2) = 3."
    }
   },
